  on the reality of things and i had that  hammered into me again in the job that i  had before i was lucky enough to be able  to have this be my living which was that  i worked in sales and every year i had  to give a projection for what i expected  my sales numbers to be  and every year i had the same back and  forth which is i made a realistic  projection of what i actually  realistically thought i could do maybe  even cushion a little more thinking well  maybe i'll get lucky and do a little bit  better than i think and then every year  it will come back and they'd say this  has to be a hundred thousand dollars  higher this has to we have to round this  up to the next million this has to be  more and i always had effectively the  same conversation which is so you want  me to lie to you  because you want me to put down a number  that i don't think i can hit simply  because that's what you have to tell  investors to keep them happy  because this is the thing you have to  realize investors have not only more  power more clout they have also way more  protections than consumers do certainly  than employees do  capitalism sucks if you weren't aware  but anyways let's get into this a little  bit more  so with something like netflix i said  they were always going to have this  problem because  there's a limited number of people on  the planet just logistically eventually  you are going to have everyone who can  be subscribed to you subscribe to you  and if you're thinking well they've got  like what 200 some odd million  subscribers there's almost 8 billion  people on this planet they've got plenty  of room for growth yeah but okay  let's actually break that down because  it's not every single person that is a  viable customer it has to be someone who  has reliable electricity a television  internet access internet access that is  high enough speed to be able to do  streaming they have to be able to afford  that internet access they have to have  enough disposable incom